<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Custom theme attributes for the pastel stat pills.

    **Why these exist.** Material 3 ships a fixed set of colour *roles* — primary, secondary,
    tertiary, error and the surfaces. Those answer the question "how important is this?", and the
    app already uses them for exactly that. A stat pill asks a different question: a row showing
    age, weight and species needs three colours that are *distinguishable from one another*, all
    equally quiet, none of them implying one fact matters more than the next. There is no Material
    role for "one of several equal categories", so the app declares its own.

    **Why attributes rather than colours.** A layout could name @color/pill_light_mint directly, but
    that colour is only correct in light mode — the pill would keep its pale wash on a dark
    background and the text on it would vanish. Declaring an *attribute* here and giving it a value
    in both values/themes.xml and values-night/themes.xml means a layout writes
    ?attr/colorPillMint once and gets the right value in either theme, which is the same mechanism
    the Material roles themselves use.

    Each pill has a pair: the background, and the text colour guaranteed legible on it. They are
    always used together — text on colorPillMint must be colorOnPillMint and nothing else.
-->
<resources>

    <!--
        The accent as a *foreground* colour, for text and icons that should read as accented.

        Declared for the same reason as the pills: Material has no role for it. colorPrimary is the
        accent, but this app's accent is a bright yellow chosen to be filled behind dark text — and
        it is far too light to *be* text on a white card. colorOnPrimary is the wrong answer too,
        since that colour is guaranteed legible on the yellow, not on the surface.

        So the app declares its own, a deeper amber of the same hue. See colors.xml for the values
        and the contrast reasoning.
    -->
    <attr name="colorAccentText" format="color" />

    <!--
        The "this one is chosen" fill, used on the Tasks tab for the selected day in the week strip,
        the selected filter chips, and the Delegate button.

        Declared because no Material role means "selected, strongly". In the light theme it is the
        app's near-black with the yellow accent written on it; in the dark theme a near-black fill
        would vanish into the near-black page, so the pair flips to a yellow fill with dark text. Both
        are the highest-contrast thing on their page, which is the property that actually matters.

        colorOnSelectedContainer is the accented text on that fill (yellow on black / dark on yellow).
        colorOnSelectedContainerNeutral is plain text on the same fill (white on black / dark on
        yellow), for a button label that should read as a word rather than as a highlight.
    -->
    <attr name="colorSelectedContainer" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnSelectedContainer" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnSelectedContainerNeutral" format="color" />

    <!--
        The *unavailable* version of that same strong fill, used by the sign-in screens' main button
        while it cannot be pressed yet ("VERIFY CODE" before all six digits are in).

        Declared because Material's own disabled treatment — the enabled colour at low opacity — turns
        a near-black button into a washed-out grey smear on the cream page, which reads as broken
        rather than as "not yet". The design instead shows a solid charcoal with pale text: clearly the
        same button, clearly resting. In dark mode the enabled fill is yellow, so the resting fill is a
        muted charcoal a step above the page rather than a dim yellow.
    -->
    <attr name="colorDisabledContainer" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnDisabledContainer" format="color" />

    <!-- Mint — the app uses this one for a pet's age. -->
    <attr name="colorPillMint" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnPillMint" format="color" />

    <!-- Sky — weight. -->
    <attr name="colorPillSky" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnPillSky" format="color" />

    <!-- Lilac — species. -->
    <attr name="colorPillLilac" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnPillLilac" format="color" />

    <!-- Peach — outstanding care. -->
    <attr name="colorPillPeach" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnPillPeach" format="color" />

    <!-- Pale green — the profile screen's "VERIFIED" pill. Not part of the four-hue stat-pill family
         above, which exists so three facts side by side can be told apart; this one always means the
         same thing, and green is what it means. It is exposed the same way for the same reason: the
         light and dark values are not a lighter and darker version of each other, so a layout has to
         reach them through a role rather than naming a colour. -->
    <attr name="colorPillVerified" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnPillVerified" format="color" />

    <!--
        The log in screen's wrong-password warning cards: the yellow one for attempts 1–3, the red one
        for the last attempt before sign-in locks.

        Exposed as roles for the same reason as the pills above: each card's light and dark values are
        not a lighter and darker version of one another — the light theme washes the page colour, the
        dark theme darkens it — so a layout has to ask for the role rather than name a colour. Three
        roles per card, because a card is a fill, an edge and (for the red one) a title colour that all
        have to move together; see the log in section of colors.xml for the values.
    -->
    <attr name="colorAuthAlertWarning" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorAuthAlertWarningEdge" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorAuthAlertDanger" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorAuthAlertDangerEdge" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnAuthAlertDanger" format="color" />

    <!--
        ===================== The floating bottom navigation bar =====================

        Six roles for one control, which needs a word of justification given how few things in the
        app declare any.

        The bar is drawn almost entirely from *drawables* — a rounded slab, a pill behind the active
        tab, a rounded badge, plus two colour state lists for the ink on them. A drawable cannot ask
        "which theme am I in?"; it can only name a colour or read a theme attribute. So every value
        in the bar that differs between light and dark has to be reachable as an attribute, or the
        whole set would have to be duplicated into res/drawable-night and res/color-night — six more
        files saying the same thing twice, and six more chances for one to be updated and its twin
        forgotten.

        None of the six maps onto a Material role. colorSurface has no alpha and the slab needs one;
        colorPrimary is the accent, but the *active pill* is near-black by day and the accent only at
        night, which is the exact inverse; and Material has no role at all for "an alert count drawn
        inside the control it belongs to". See the bottom-navigation section of colors.xml for the
        values and the contrast reasoning behind them.
    -->

    <!-- The translucent slab the bar is drawn on. -->
    <attr name="colorNavBarSurface" format="color" />

    <!-- The active tab's pill, and the icon and label written on it. Always used as a pair: ink that
         is not colorOnNavTabActive is not guaranteed legible on the pill. -->
    <attr name="colorNavTabActive" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnNavTabActive" format="color" />

    <!-- The four inactive tabs' icons — a solid value, never the active ink at reduced opacity; see
         colors.xml for why that distinction is load-bearing here. -->
    <attr name="colorNavTabMuted" format="color" />

    <!-- The open-task badge as it appears *on the active pill*. Its inactive colours are fixed rather
         than themed (a red dot with white on it), so they need no roles. -->
    <attr name="colorNavBadgeActive" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnNavBadgeActive" format="color" />

    <!--
        ============================= The map cards =============================

        One role, shared by the Places tab's map card and the Add a place screen's.

        It is here rather than named as a colour in the two layouts for the reason this file exists at
        all: it is a light/dark *pair* that is not a lighter and darker version of the other — a pale
        sage ground becomes a deep slate one — so a layout has to reach it through a role.

        What it colours is the ground a map card shows *while the map loads*: the WebView inside is
        transparent and stays invisible until its first page has been drawn and tinted, so for the
        second or two before tiles arrive this is the whole card. See MapEmbedView.
    -->
    <attr name="colorMapGround" format="color" />

    <!--
        ====================== The vaccination status pills ======================

        Three pairs, one per state of a pet's next due date: clear (green), overdue (red) and due
        soon (the accent). Each is a fill with the ink guaranteed legible on it, and the two are
        always used together — ink that is not the partner of its fill is not guaranteed to be
        readable on it.

        The first two are here for the reason every role in this file is: each is a light/dark pair
        that is not a lighter and darker version of the other — a pale wash with dark ink becomes a
        deep ground with pale ink — so a layout cannot name a colour and has to ask for a role. No
        Material role fits, either: colorError is a validation red tuned for a line of text, and there
        is no role at all meaning "this appointment has been missed".

        **The third pair does not change between the themes, and is still declared here.** By the rule
        the rest of this file follows it could be named as a colour directly. It is a role anyway so
        that a status is *one kind of lookup*: ui/pet/VaccinationStatus.kt stores an attribute per
        state and the adapter resolves it the same way for all three, rather than branching on which
        states happen to be themed — a branch that would have to be revisited the moment a fourth
        state were added. See the vaccination section of colors.xml for the values.
    -->
    <attr name="colorVaccinationClear" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnVaccinationClear" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorVaccinationOverdue" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnVaccinationOverdue" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorVaccinationDue" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnVaccinationDue" format="color" />

</resources>
